    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large language models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and thus individual layers' compute) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts in full; tokens are routed to whichever device hosts the expert that the router selected.\n\nTP is the workhorse for dense models and for the attention/shared layers of MoE models. EP is essential for MoE models where the expert FFN parameters dominate memory (e.g., models with dozens to hundreds of experts per layer). In practice, large MoE deployments combine both: TP\u00d72 or TP\u00d74 for attention and shared components, EP\u00d78 or EP\u00d716 for the expert layers, often with data parallelism (DP) layered on top for throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The two dominant matrix multiplies are the attention projection and the MLP. The canonical Megatron-style sharding:\n\n**MLP block** (input `X` of shape `[B, S, H]`, hidden dim `H`, FFN dim `F`):\n\n- First GEMM: `Y = X \u00b7 W1` where `W1 \u2208 R^{H\u00d7F}` is **column-parallel** \u2014 split along `F`. Each device computes `Y_i = X \u00b7 W1_i` of shape `[B, S, F/P]`. No communication needed before this GEMM since `X` is replicated.\n- Nonlinearity (GEL",
